+
+# Decision: Preview cache eviction policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private cache of rendered JPEG contact sheets, bounded at 12 GiB of cached bytes. Workloads alternate between two shapes: long sequential screenings that touch thousands of distinct clips once, and comparison sessions that repeatedly revisit a small set of clips. The eviction policy decides what survives a screening, and the working policy also determines how much bookkeeping the worker carries and what gets tuned afterward. Choosing wrong in either direction is visible to editors: a comparison sheet that must re-render costs the editor a ~450 ms wait plus a pending-response round trip; a policy that never lets go of old favorites wastes the byte budget on sheets no one requests anymore.
+
+All replacement state is private to the preview worker (tenet: *keep cache paths and replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker*), so this decision binds only the worker's internals. The external contract — 200 with JPEG bytes, 202 with a job ticket and a two-second retry interval, 404 for an unknown revision — is fixed regardless of the policy chosen here.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Gate — external surface unchanged.** The policy may not alter the HTTP contract, the render queue's four-job concurrency, or the 12 GiB bound, and may not expose any cache or policy state to other nodes. This gate is inherited from the HLD's fixed-interface goal and the privacy tenet.
+- **Weighted criterion — retention of reused sheets under screening scans (major).** A long sequential screening must not evict the small working set that comparison sessions keep revisiting. This is the workload failure the cache most needs to survive, and it leans on the tenet *a cache miss is ordinary behavior, not an API failure* — misses are tolerable individually, but a policy that manufactures them in bursts from one editor's scan is the failure mode.
+- **Weighted criterion — bookkeeping cost (moderate).** Per-request metadata work on the hot path (every hit and admission) should stay small; the pilot runs 24 editors on one eight-core worker.
+- **Weighted criterion — graceful staleness (moderate).** When editors switch projects, yesterday's popular sheets should become evictable within normal traffic rather than requiring explicit invalidation or counter resets.
+- **Weighted criterion — tunability (minor).** The policy should expose at most one free parameter, adjustable against screening logs after the pilot, rather than a family of constants.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description.** A single LRU-ordered list over all cached sheet entries. Every hit moves the entry to the head; eviction takes the tail. Bookkeeping is one list update per hit.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gate (external surface unchanged): **passes** — no new state or interfaces.
+- Retention under scans (major): **weak** — a sequential scan of thousands of one-use sheets walks the entire list, evicting precisely the reused working set at the tail.
+- Bookkeeping cost (moderate): **strong** — the cheapest of the three; one pointer update per hit.
+- Graceful staleness (moderate): **strong** — recency tracks the current project naturally; stale favorites age out fastest here.
+- Tunability (minor): **strong** — no parameters at all.
+
+**Summary.** Cheapest and most adaptive, but fails exactly the workload the cache exists to protect.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description.** Each entry carries a hit counter, periodically halved (aged) so counts reflect recent popularity rather than lifetime totals. Eviction takes the lowest-count entry; ties broken by age.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gate (external surface unchanged): **passes** — counters stay inside the worker.
+- Retention under scans (major): **strong** — one scan pass cannot displace a sheet that has accumulated hits across sessions.
+- Bookkeeping cost (moderate): **weak** — counter reads and writes on every hit plus a periodic aging pass over all entries.
+- Graceful staleness (moderate): **weak** — yesterday's favorites retain high aged counts and can hold their bytes long after the project switch; aging rate becomes a second parameter to get right.
+- Tunability (minor): **partial** — the aging interval and decay factor are two constants to reason about.
+
+**Summary.** Best raw retention of popular sheets, but the cost lands on the hot path and staleness lags a project switch.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description.** Two ordered queues over cached entries. A0 is a FIFO of first-use entries: a sheet admitted for a key with no prior cached admission enters A0's tail. A1 is an LRU list of reused entries: a hit on an A0 entry promotes it to A1's head, and a hit within A1 refreshes it to A1's head. Eviction takes A0's tail first, and A1's LRU tail only while A0 is empty. The A1/A0 entry-count split (75%/25% initially) is a tuning parameter; the 12 GiB byte bound is enforced independently — queues only order eviction candidates.
+
+**Demonstration.** A 3,000-clip screening scan fills A0 (~1,500 entries at a 25% split of a ~6,000-entry cache) and evicts only other one-use sheets; the ~500-sheet comparison working set sits in A1 and is untouched. A comparison sheet hit once during the scan is promoted to A1 on its second request and thereafter protected.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gate (external surface unchanged): **passes** — queues and promotion state are entirely internal; byte-bound admission and the API contract are unchanged.
+- Retention under scans (major): **strong** — scan traffic is absorbed and evicted within A0; reused sheets are structurally out of reach until A0 is empty.
+- Bookkeeping cost (moderate): **partial** — two queues with tail/head operations per hit; more than LRU, no periodic global pass, no per-entry counters.
+- Graceful staleness (moderate): **strong** — after a project switch, unrevisited sheets fall out of A1 by LRU order as new traffic promotes entries; nothing freezes them in place.
+- Tunability (minor): **partial** — one split parameter, tunable against screening logs.
+
+**Summary.** Scan protection comparable to frequency counting at close to LRU's hot-path cost, paid for with a second queue and one tunable split.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: 2Q.** The major criterion decides it: LRU is the only option that fails retention of reused sheets under screening scans, and that failure is the workload the cache is bounded and deduplicated to serve. Frequency counting matches 2Q's protection but puts counter maintenance and an aging pass on the hot path and keeps stale favorites warm across project switches; 2Q reaches the same protection through queue structure, whose extra cost is bounded and internal. The tunable split defers precision to pilot logs rather than guessing it up front. All options pass the external-surface gate, so no downstream contract changes; the obligations this choice creates — two queues, promotion bookkeeping, and split tuning — live entirely inside the preview worker.
+
+Turns on the tenet *a cache miss is ordinary behavior, not an API failure*: the policy is judged on the burst of avoidable re-renders it would inflict, not on any single miss.
