 ## Architecture
 
@@ -23,4 +23,29 @@ Resolve the immutable source revision before rendering. The cache key includes c
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses the **2Q** scheme: two ordered queues over the cached sheet entries, distinct from the render queue.
+
+- **A0 (first-use queue, FIFO).** A freshly admitted sheet — a preview key with no prior cached admission — enters at the tail of A0. A0 absorbs sequential screening scans: a long pass over many clips fills A0 and evicts other one-use sheets, leaving reused sheets untouched.
+- **A1 (reuse queue, LRU).** When a request hits an entry still in A0 — evidence the sheet was requested again — that entry is promoted to the head of A1. A hit on an entry already in A1 moves it to A1's head. A1 therefore holds exactly the comparison working set the screenings keep revisiting.
+- **Victim selection.** When eviction is needed (per the 12 GiB admission bound), the victim is the tail of A0; if A0 is empty, it is the LRU tail of A1. Eviction order never reaches an entry whose render job is still pending — such entries do not yet hold cached bytes.
+
+The queue split — the fraction of cache entries A1 may occupy before its own overflow contributes victims — is a tuning parameter, not a partition of the 12 GiB byte budget: the byte bound is enforced exactly as before, and the queues only order candidates for eviction. The initial split is 75% A1 / 25% A0, to be tuned against observed screening logs. All queues and promotion bookkeeping are private to this worker.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (A0 FIFO first-use queue + A1 LRU reuse queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU lets one long sequential screening evict the actively reused comparison sheets, which is the workload this cache most needs to survive. Aged frequency counts retain repeatedly popular clips but add per-hit counter and aging maintenance and keep stale favorites warm after a project switch. 2Q protects the reused working set from scan floods; its cost — two queues and a tunable split — is contained entirely inside the worker, and the split is tunable against screening logs. Full record: `decisions/eviction-policy-2q.md`. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy is 2Q with an initial 75% A1 / 25% A0 queue split; the split is a tuning parameter, not a byte partition.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Tune the A1/A0 split against observed screening logs once the pilot has real traffic.
+2. Whether A0 should keep a non-resident directory of recently evicted keys (classic 2Q variant) to catch a second request arriving just after eviction — defer until hit logs show it matters.
+
